WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.830
(dramatic music)

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:04.140
- [Narrator] This tiny strip
of land in Eastern Europe

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.720
is being called NATO's danger spot.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.540
It's known as the Suwalki Gap.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.790
It forms a border between NATO members,

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:14.010
Poland and Lithuania,

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:18.000
and lies in between two
Russian military strongholds.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.267
Military strategists say,

00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:21.330
"This sparsely populated area

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.850
with hills, forests, and small villages

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.470
will be a prime target

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:29.010
if Russia decides to
attack NATO territory."

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:31.080
Poland and Lithuania are worried about

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.940
this scenario becoming reality

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.370
as fighters from the
Wagner Mercenary Group

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.460
arrive on their doorstep in Belarus.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:39.960
So here's what's at stake

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.603
if the Suwalki Gap comes under attack.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.310
The Suwalki Gap is around 60 miles long

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.560
and separates Poland to the west

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.420
and Lithuania to the east,

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.580
to its northwest, lies Kaliningrad

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.140
a chunk of Russian territory

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.840
with a number of military bases.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.600
It's also home to Russia's
Baltic Naval Fleet

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.610
and its only ports on the Baltic Sea

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.470
that doesn't freeze up in the winter,

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.800
but it's not linked to
the Russian mainland.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.590
The most direct route
from Kaliningrad to Russia

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.300
will be through the Suwalki Gap

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.360
via Russia's neighbor and ally Belarus,

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.790
but the Russian President's
Alexander Lukashenko

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.670
allowed Moscow to use
his country as a base

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.403
to launch the full-scale
invasion of Ukraine.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.470
Military analysts say,

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.740
Russia could attack from
two ends of the Suwalki Gap

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.690
with soldiers from Kaliningrad

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.420
and forces advancing through Belarus,

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.010
which would leave NATO members,

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.550
Lithuania, Latvia, and Estonia isolated.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.940
Moscow would also gain more
access to the Baltic Sea,

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.610
a crucial gateway for commercial shipping.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:49.020
NATO has fewer options to defend
its Baltic members by land,

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.820
they'll have to send in reinforcements

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.660
through Poland to Lithuania
by crossing the Suwalki Gap

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.820
only two highways and a train line

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.460
connect Poland and the Baltic States.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.350
That means this narrow strip of land

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:05.430
could become congested with
troops and military hardware.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:09.120
The stakes were raised even
higher in recent months.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.760
First, fighters from the
Wagner Mercenary Group

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.920
started relocating to Belarus

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.103
after aborting a mutiny in Russia in June.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.587
Then, according to Belarusian television,

00:02:20.587 --> 00:02:23.790
"Wagner troops began
training Belarusian soldiers,

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.400
just three miles from the Polish border."

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.907
Poland's Prime Minister says,

00:02:27.907 --> 00:02:30.756
"The situation is becoming dangerous."

00:02:30.756 --> 00:02:34.673
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.340
- [Narrator] Warsaw responded by sending

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.380
up to 10,000 extra troops

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:48.600
to guard its border with Belarus.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.940
Poland and Lithuania have also closed

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.890
some of their border crossings
with their eastern neighbor.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:58.830
NATO has tried to strengthen
its defenses in the region.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:00.180
- We're set and ready.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.770
- [Narrator] Multinational battle groups

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.290
with around 1,000 troops have been set up

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.328
in each of the Baltic States.

00:03:06.328 --> 00:03:07.350
(artillery blasting)

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.330
Large-scale drills have taken place

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.490
in the area close to the Suwalki Gap

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.957
as a way to show NATO's readiness.

00:03:13.957 --> 00:03:14.790
(helicopter blades whirling)

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.170
The Baltic States and Poland

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.600
have also ramped up defense spending.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:19.500
They have all vowed

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:23.610
to spend 3% of their
GDP per year on defense,

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:26.490
more than NATO's 2% target.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.520
The leaders of Lithuania,
Latvia, and Estonia

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.750
want NATO forces to be stationed
in the region permanently.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:34.583
In the meantime,

00:03:34.583 --> 00:03:37.620
NATO's defense capabilities
in the area strengthened

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.040
with two new members, Finland and Sweden.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.790
This opens up a gateway to
reach the Baltics by sea and air

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.700
boosting any potential defense on land.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.410
Finland and Sweden's militaries

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:52.350
also add hundreds of warplanes, tanks,

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:55.685
and tens of thousands of
soldiers to the alliance.

00:03:55.685 --> 00:03:56.820
(gunfire blasting)

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.280
Analysts say, "This
makes a Russian invasion

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.110
even more costly."

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:02.940
Finland and Sweden's integration,

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:06.780
means Kaliningrad is
effectively encircled by NATO.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.810
Any attack by Russia could
leave the Exclave vulnerable

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.903
to isolation and blockade by the alliance.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.630
Analysts believe Russia

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:19.110
is unlikely to launch an armed
attack on the Suwalki Gap

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.800
while he has his hands full with Ukraine.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:22.980
(crowd cheering)

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.260
Polish opposition leader, Donald Tusk,

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.900
who's campaigning to become
the next prime minister,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:28.957
accused the governments,

00:04:28.957 --> 00:04:30.930
"Of using the Wagner threats

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:34.080
to stoke fear ahead of
October's election."

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.393
But Poland's governments denies this.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.357
Still, NATO's Eastern members say,

00:04:40.357 --> 00:04:42.300
"Wagner's presence on their doorstep

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.580
remains a security risk.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.130
That's why they want to
make sure the Suwalki Gap

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:48.480
is well defended

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.455
to deter any potential
aggression from Russia."

00:04:52.455 --> 00:04:55.205
(dramatic music)

